# Momus brain settings. Copy to .env (never commit .env) and change what you need; every value below is the
# default (an empty value means the default), which is safe for a single user on this machine. After a change,
# recreate the brain so that it reads the file again: docker compose up -d --force-recreate brain
# Every setting of every container is described in docs/configuration.md.

# Addresses the browser uses to reach the brain, comma separated. Every state-changing request must come from one
# of these (Origin check). Behind a reverse proxy, put the public https:// address here; then MOMUS_COOKIE_SECURE must be
# 1 (the brain refuses to start otherwise) and MOMUS_TRUSTED_PROXY should be set (the brain warns at start and on the
# Settings page otherwise).
MOMUS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://127.0.0.1:8080,http://localhost:8080
# 1 = mark the session cookie Secure (and name it __Host-...). Set this when the brain is served over TLS.
MOMUS_COOKIE_SECURE=0
# A session ends after this many minutes without a request (5 to 1440).
MOMUS_SESSION_IDLE_MINUTES=30
# A session ends this many hours after sign-in, whatever its activity (1 to 168).
MOMUS_SESSION_MAX_HOURS=12
# Minimum password length (12 to 128).
MOMUS_PASSWORD_MIN_LENGTH=12
# Sign-in limits. Every failed sign-in counts for 24 hours, against the username typed (whether or not that account
# exists) and against the client address (an IPv6 client by its /64). After this many failures for a username (3 to
# 50), or twice this many from one address, each further attempt has to wait: 15 seconds after the last failure,
# doubling with each further failure, up to MOMUS_LOGIN_MAX_DELAY_SECONDS. An attempt refused while waiting is not
# recorded and does not extend the wait. A successful sign-in clears nothing: a count goes down only when its failures
# are 24 hours old, or when the host recovery command below clears it, and the owner's own typos count too. So real and
# unknown usernames get the same answers, with one exception: a client that holds a username at its limit sees the
# effect of other people's failed attempts on that name (its wait grows), and of a sign-in on it being checked for a
# moment. Only a real account's owner produces those, so such a client can tell a real username whose owner has just
# mistyped or is signing in from an unknown one. (MOMUS_LOGIN_WINDOW_MINUTES is not a setting; if it is set, the brain
# logs that it is ignored.)
MOMUS_LOGIN_MAX_FAILURES=5
# The longest wait between sign-in attempts, in seconds (30 to 3600). The admin can always sign in after at most this
# long, unless an attacker's own attempt gets in first the moment the wait ends: an attacker who keeps failing on the
# admin's username keeps it at the cap. On the host, two recovery commands (no web route reaches them; each writes an
# audit entry with the actor host-admin):
#   docker compose exec brain python -B -m tools.admin clear-login-delays [username]   end the delay now (one username, or all)
#   docker compose exec brain python -B -m tools.admin reset-password <username>        new password read from standard input;
#                                                                                        ends that account's sessions
# With a username, clear-login-delays clears only that username's failures; failures recorded for other (for example
# made-up) usernames still hold the per-address delay, and it says how many remain. Without a username it clears all.
MOMUS_LOGIN_MAX_DELAY_SECONDS=300
# Password hashes computed at once (1 to 8). Each needs 32 MiB; further sign-ins wait up to 1 s, then are refused
# with "The server is busy".
MOMUS_LOGIN_HASH_CONCURRENCY=2
# What a flood of sign-ins can still do. A client with many source addresses (many IPv4 addresses, or many IPv6 /64s)
# gets 10 guesses per address and 5 per username before any delay. Enough such requests keep both hash slots taken,
# so real sign-ins get "The server is busy" for as long as the flood lasts. There is deliberately no global ceiling
# that refuses sign-ins, because that would make locking everyone out cheaper. The audit log does not grow one row per
# attempt during a flood: above 30 failed sign-ins in one minute (all clients together; fixed, auth.FLOOD_PER_MINUTE),
# no individual login-failed or login-delay entry is written for the rest of that minute, and one login-flood entry
# is written once it is over: the minute's count, every existing account tried in it with its failures (up to 20), and
# the ten other usernames and ten source addresses (an IPv6 client by its /64) with the most failures. The attempt rows
# the limits need are still written; they are deleted after 24 hours. Put the UI behind a network control (a VPN, an
# allow-list at the proxy or firewall), not on the open internet.
#
# Off (empty) by default. Behind a reverse proxy, set this to that proxy's one IP address as the brain sees it. Only
# requests whose connection comes from exactly that address have their X-Forwarded-For honored: the last entry, the
# client address the proxy appended, is then used for the per-address sign-in limit and the audit log. From any other
# address the header is ignored. The address must not change: Compose gives a container a new address when it is
# recreated, so give the proxy a fixed one on a network with a fixed subnet, for example in compose.override.yaml:
#   networks:  { brain_net: { ipam: { config: [ { subnet: 172.30.81.0/24 } ] } } }
#   services:  { proxy: { networks: { brain_net: { ipv4_address: 172.30.81.10 } } } }
# and MOMUS_TRUSTED_PROXY=172.30.81.10. A value that is not one IP address (a name, range or list) leaves the setting
# off, and a value that stops matching the proxy's address turns the feature off: every client then shares the proxy's
# address for the sign-in limits, and the brain logs once per start that a request carrying X-Forwarded-For did not
# come from MOMUS_TRUSTED_PROXY. If the proxy's last X-Forwarded-For entry is not a bare address
# (brackets, a port, "unknown"), the proxy's address is used and the brain logs that once per start.
MOMUS_TRUSTED_PROXY=
# Every request's queries on the scan snapshot stop after this many seconds (1 to 60); the page then says so.
MOMUS_SNAPSHOT_QUERY_SECONDS=2
# The largest scan snapshot the brain accepts, in MiB (16 to 4096).
MOMUS_SNAPSHOT_MAX_MB=512
# 1 = run the schedules inside the brain (daily recheck, daily version watch, monthly full run); 0 = never queue scheduled jobs.
MOMUS_SCHEDULER=1
# Inside the container: where the app database lives (a volume), where job files go, where results are read from.
MOMUS_DATA_DIR=/data
MOMUS_JOBS_DIR=/srv/jobs
MOMUS_PUBLISHED_DIR=/srv/published
# Address and port the brain listens on inside its container (Compose publishes it on 127.0.0.1:8080 only).
MOMUS_LISTEN_HOST=0.0.0.0
MOMUS_PORT=8080
# The scanner's settings are NOT read from this file. Set them in the scanner service's "environment:" block in
# compose.yaml (or a compose.override.yaml); docs/configuration.md describes each one:
#   SCAN_JOB_MAX_AGE_HOURS=72   the scanner refuses (final status "rejected", nothing run, no scope written) a job whose
#                               created_at is older than this many hours (1 to 8760). A job the scanner has finished is
#                               never run again in any case: it keeps its own record of finished job ids in its data
#                               volume, and the brain removes each job file once its status is final.
#   SCAN_KEEP_SNAPSHOTS=10      snapshots kept in the published folder, the current one included
#   SCAN_POLL_SECONDS=5         seconds between looks at the jobs folder when there is no work
#   SCAN_ROOT, SCAN_JOBS, SCAN_PUBLISHED   the scanner's folders inside its container (match the volume mounts)
#   SCAN_LAB, SCAN_LAB_CIDR     lab mode and its one range; set only by compose.lab.yaml
# Lab mode is set by compose.lab.yaml only (with the demo target). Leave these empty for real use. Lab mode and the
# lab range come only from these two settings; the scanner's heartbeat is shown but never switches lab mode on.
MOMUS_LAB=0
MOMUS_LAB_CIDR=

# ---- The analyst (AI model provider). Off by default; everything else works without it. See docs/analyst.md.
# Only these MOMUS_ANALYST_* settings are read (and, for bedrock without a key, the standard AWS credential variables).
# The SDKs' own variables have no effect: keys, base URLs and endpoints are passed explicitly, the HTTP clients trust
# no proxy or CA-bundle variable and follow no redirect, and at start-up the brain removes from its environment the
# variables an SDK would read anyway (ANTHROPIC_*, OPENAI_*, GOOGLE_GENAI_*, the proxy, CA-bundle and SSLKEYLOGFILE
# variables, AWS_ENDPOINT_URL*, and more: docs/analyst.md lists them) and logs their names. Before the first run an
# admin confirms on the Settings page what is sent to which provider; that is asked again whenever the text of that
# statement changes (provider, region, model, metadata-only, automatic brief, proxy).
# none (default), anthropic, openai, gemini, bedrock. (mock and susceptible are test providers, lab mode only.)
MOMUS_ANALYST_PROVIDER=none
# The provider's model id (configuration only; check the provider's model list).
MOMUS_ANALYST_MODEL=
# The key, or (better) a file holding it, e.g. a Docker secret: MOMUS_ANALYST_API_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/momus_analyst_api_key
# Required for anthropic, openai and gemini. For bedrock it is optional and means a Bedrock API key; without it the
# default AWS credential chain is used (environment, shared files, container or instance role).
MOMUS_ANALYST_API_KEY=
MOMUS_ANALYST_API_KEY_FILE=
# Bedrock only, required (e.g. us-east-1). The endpoint is built from it: https://bedrock-runtime.<region>.amazonaws.com.
MOMUS_ANALYST_REGION=
# Optional egress proxy for the provider requests: http(s)://host:port, no user name, password or path. An invalid
# value stops the analyst (it never goes direct instead). The proxy sees which provider is contacted, not the content:
# TLS inspection by the proxy is not supported. It is named in the statement an admin confirms.
MOMUS_ANALYST_PROXY=
# 1 = send no text taken from scanned sites, no accepted notes and no reasons people typed for marks: identifiers,
# IP addresses, severities, versions, counts only.
MOMUS_ANALYST_METADATA_ONLY=0
# 1 = write a brief automatically for each new scan snapshot.
MOMUS_ANALYST_AUTO_BRIEF=0
# One provider request, in seconds (10 to 600).
MOMUS_ANALYST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=120
# Limits per run and per day, all enforced in code and recorded with each run.
MOMUS_ANALYST_MAX_STEPS=12
MOMUS_ANALYST_MAX_TOKENS_PER_CALL=16000
MOMUS_ANALYST_RUN_TOKEN_BUDGET=200000
MOMUS_ANALYST_DAILY_TOKEN_BUDGET=1000000
MOMUS_ANALYST_RUN_SECONDS=300
MOMUS_ANALYST_MAX_PROPOSALS=10
MOMUS_ANALYST_MAX_INVALID=2
# Accepted notes included in each prompt, newest first, at most this many characters (0 to 20000).
MOMUS_ANALYST_MEMORY_CHARS=4000
# 1 = store each prompt and response with its run, for debugging. Never in lab mode.
MOMUS_ANALYST_DEBUG=0

# ---- The seeder (optional DNS seeding container; Compose profile "seed"). See docs/data-flows.md.
# These SEED_* settings belong in a separate file, seed.env, next to compose.yaml (never commit it). The seeder reads
# seed.env only, never .env, so it never receives the brain's settings or the analyst's key; the brain ignores SEED_*.
# Every source is off until set here. Only domains the scanner has DNS-verified are seeded: the scanner writes their
# names to verified_domains.json in the seed_scope volume, the only thing the seeder reads from the scanner (it does not
# mount the published folder). The seeder contacts only the DNS services set here; a transfer server is usually the
# zone's own name server, which can be a host in scope, and the scanner's firewall and exclusions do not apply to it.
# 0 or empty: run once and exit (docker compose --profile seed run --rm seeder); 15 to 10080: run again every N minutes.
SEED_INTERVAL_MINUTES=
# Route 53: 1 = read public hosted zones under verified domains through the default AWS credential chain (on an AWS
# host the instance role; elsewhere a static credentials file given as a Docker secret, named by
# AWS_SHARED_CREDENTIALS_FILE=/run/secrets/momus_seed_aws_credentials). No profile, no single sign-on, no credential cache.
# Only the standard AWS partition is supported (not China or GovCloud): the clients use route53.amazonaws.com and
# sts.<region>.amazonaws.com, given explicitly; endpoint, proxy, CA-bundle and plugin variables (AWS_ENDPOINT_URL*,
# HTTPS_PROXY, AWS_CA_BUNDLE and the like) are removed at start-up and logged by name. The credential variables stay.
SEED_ROUTE53=
# Route 53, optional: one IAM role to assume first (arn:aws:iam::<account>:role/<name>), e.g. a read-only DNS role.
SEED_ROUTE53_ROLE_ARN=
# Route 53, optional: the region for the role assumption (default us-east-1). Route 53 itself is global.
SEED_ROUTE53_REGION=
# Zone files: 1 = read zone files or plain host lists from ./seed-zones (mounted read-only at /srv/seed-zones). A file
# larger than 16 MiB is skipped; a PowerDNS answer larger than 8 MiB is refused (pdns-answer-too-large). The seeder's
# memory is capped at 512 MiB in compose.yaml.
SEED_ZONE_FILES=
# Zone transfers: space-separated zone@address pairs, the server as an IP address (never looked up by name), e.g.
# example.com@192.0.2.53. The server must allow transfers to this machine's address.
SEED_AXFR=
# PowerDNS HTTP API: base URL (http or https, no user name, query or fragment), server id (default localhost), zones.
# With http the API key crosses the network in clear text: use https unless the path to PowerDNS is a private network
# you trust. The seeder logs a warning at start for an http URL whose host is not loopback.
SEED_PDNS_URL=
SEED_PDNS_SERVER=
SEED_PDNS_ZONES=
# PowerDNS key: a file holding it, normally a Docker secret (default /run/secrets/momus_seed_pdns_api_key). A key in a
# variable is not accepted.
SEED_PDNS_API_KEY_FILE=
